WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.375 --> 00:00:08.394
The care you provide is only as good as
the documentation that supports it.

2
00:00:08.625 --> 00:00:12.545
This lesson is about soap notes and soapy
air documentation,

3
00:00:13.224 --> 00:00:17.005
the structured format that respiratory
therapists use to record assessments,

4
00:00:17.685 --> 00:00:20.425
communicate findings, and justify every
clinical decision.

5
00:00:20.833 --> 00:00:24.193
SOAP stands for Subjective, Objective,
Assessment,

6
00:00:24.473 --> 00:00:24.833
and Plan.

7
00:00:25.167 --> 00:00:29.127
SOAPIER adds Implementing, Evaluating, and
Reassessing.

8
00:00:29.416 --> 00:00:32.816
Together they form a complete picture of a
patient encounter from the first question

9
00:00:32.816 --> 00:00:35.316
you ask to the final modification you make
to their treatment.

10
00:00:35.791 --> 00:00:39.371
The subjective section captures what the
patient tells you, including

11
00:00:39.371 --> 00:00:41.591
their words, their symptoms, and their

12
00:00:41.591 --> 00:00:41.891
concerns.

13
00:00:42.416 --> 00:00:45.716
Never let your interpretation replace
their voice in this section.

14
00:00:46.791 --> 00:00:49.831
The objective section is where your
measurable data lives.

15
00:00:50.333 --> 00:00:52.933
This includes vital signs, breath sounds,

16
00:00:53.533 --> 00:00:57.013
SpO2, ABG values, or ventilator settings.

17
00:00:57.593 --> 00:00:58.973
Anything you can observe or measure.

18
00:00:59.500 --> 00:01:00.940
No interpretations here.

19
00:01:01.291 --> 00:01:01.951
Just facts.

20
00:01:02.250 --> 00:01:04.690
The assessment is where your clinical
thinking shows.

21
00:01:05.109 --> 00:01:09.130
You're not copying the physician's
diagnosis, you're documenting your respiratory

22
00:01:09.130 --> 00:01:10.850
care interpretation of the findings.

23
00:01:11.208 --> 00:01:15.508
This could include things like moderate
secretion retention, worsening bronchospasm,

24
00:01:15.748 --> 00:01:17.408
or improved oxygenation after treatment.

25
00:01:18.666 --> 00:01:20.926
The plan connects directly to your
assessment.

26
00:01:21.583 --> 00:01:24.903
Every problem you identify needs a
corresponding action.

27
00:01:25.375 --> 00:01:29.035
The extra three letters covers the steps
when carrying out the plan.

28
00:01:29.500 --> 00:01:32.019
When implementing the plan, you complete
the necessary therapy,

29
00:01:33.019 --> 00:01:35.739
evaluate the effectiveness of the therapy,
and reassess the patient

30
00:01:35.739 --> 00:01:39.100
on how they tolerated the treatment and
any changes to the therapeutic plan.

31
00:01:41.666 --> 00:01:43.886
We'll also cover the legal dimensions of
documentation.

32
00:01:44.333 --> 00:01:46.033
The chart is a legal document.

33
00:01:46.833 --> 00:01:49.273
Late entries, corrections, and
co-signatures

34
00:01:49.273 --> 00:01:50.933
all have specific rules.

35
00:01:51.375 --> 00:01:55.755
And we'll walk through common
documentation errors that create liability and how to avoid

36
00:01:55.755 --> 00:01:55.994
them.

37
00:01:56.416 --> 00:01:58.996
By the end of this lesson, you'll be able
to

38
00:01:58.996 --> 00:02:00.676
write a complete, accurate, and legally

39
00:02:00.677 --> 00:02:03.456
sound SOAPIER note for any respiratory
care encounter.

40
00:02:03.833 --> 00:02:06.133
Good documentation doesn't just protect
you.

41
00:02:06.416 --> 00:02:07.336
It protects your patients.

42
00:02:08.249 --> 00:02:09.110
Let's dive in.

